<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maastricht</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maastricht is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in the far south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the capital of the province of Limburg. It grew from a Roman river crossing and became a centre of the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/servatius">Saint Servatius</a>, whose relics lie in the Basilica of Saint Servatius. For centuries it was ruled jointly by the dukes of Brabant and the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Liège, and it was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in 1632. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty">Maastricht Treaty</a> was signed there in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maastricht.jpg" alt="Maastricht" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Kaufmann · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maastricht_sunset.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
